## Step 2: Perform Attack Surface Reconnaissance using grep
Now that you have all the manifests saved as plain text, you can audit the entire directory simultaneously for vulnerabilities. Run these high-utility queries:
## 🔍 1. Find Unprotected Components (High Vulnerability Risk)
Look for activities, services, or receivers that set android:exported="true". If they don't require specific permissions, any malware app installed inside Waydroid can force-launch them to bypass authentication:

grep -Hn "exported" *_manifest.txt | grep "true"

## 🌐 2. Audit Custom Deep Links and URL Schemes
Attackers abuse custom deep links to hijack user sessions or steal tokens. Use this to find out what custom URLs these banking and crypto apps respond to (e.g., trust://, momo://):

grep -Hni -A 3 "scheme" *_manifest.txt

## 🛡️ 3. Check for Backup Vulnerabilities
If an app defines android:allowBackup="true", an attacker with physical access or local ADB access can extract the private local database (shared preferences, session cache) out of the container:

grep -Hn "allowBackup" *_manifest.txt | grep "true"

